{"hash":"35853606210be59d","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":109.425,"said":"Look into the sanctum, and, if the mirror above the doorway helps you, down into the small hollow in its floor. There is no tall linga here. In that hollow rise three tiny lingas, worn smooth, honoured as Brahma, Vishnu and Mahesh, and water wells up around them, held to be the Godavari herself. Even the day keeps this trinity: by the trust’s programme, the morning puja is of Brahma, the midday puja of Mahadev, and the evening puja of Vishnu. On Mondays and festivals they are covered with a golden mask and a jewelled crown that tradition says comes from the time of the Pandavas. This is the Lord who stayed because a river asked him to, for a sage who thanked even those who wronged him. Fold your hands. The tenth verse of the Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram, traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya, sings: ‘Sahyadrishirshe vimale vasantam Godavaritirapavitradeshe, yaddarshanat patakam ashu nasham prayati tam Tryambakam isham ide.’ ‘He who dwells on the pure heights of the Sahyadri, in the holy land on the Godavari’s bank, whose darshan swiftly destroys all sin: I praise that Lord, Tryambaka.’ Trimbakeshwar is the tenth Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. Now the conch sounds and the lamps rise. Let the aarti begin. Har Har Mahadev.\n\nDid you know? Because the lingas sit in a hollow below floor level, most pilgrims take darshan from the doorway, many of them through the mirror set above it.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1454},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1470,"end":1614}],"words":[[0.098,0.24,0,4],[0.35,0.192,5,9],[0.555,0.096,10,13],[0.663,0.637,14,21],[1.48,0.337,23,26],[2.009,0.12,28,30],[2.141,0.108,31,34],[2.262,0.433,35,41],[2.706,0.325,42,47],[3.043